  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should you check when imported pharmaceutical material arrives?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Check the documents against the order, the containers against the documents, and the material against its specification. Confirm identity, verify seals, labels, batch numbers and quantity, assess the transport record where storage conditions apply, and hold the whole delivery in quarantine until the quality unit releases it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is incoming goods inspection a repeat of the manufacturer's testing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The manufacturer's release testing establishes that the batch met its specification when it was made. The receipt check establishes that what arrived is that batch, in sound condition, with documents that agree. Identity testing is the part that looks inside the container; the rest is document, label and container work."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can material be moved into production while it is still in quarantine?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Quarantined material is unavailable until the quality unit releases it, and the receiving system should enforce that rather than rely on people remembering. If production needs material urgently, the answer is to prioritise the release decision, not to take the drum early and complete the paperwork afterwards."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What do you do if the certificate of analysis arrives after the goods?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Book the delivery in and hold it. The physical checks on the seal, the containers and the labels can all be done, but the comparison that matters needs the batch certificate, so release waits for it. A pattern of certificates arriving late is a supplier performance issue worth raising formally rather than absorbing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who should carry out the receipt check, the warehouse or the quality unit?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Both, with the split written down. Warehouse staff do the physical work: vehicle, seal, count, labels, container condition, and recording what they find. The quality unit reviews the documents, decides on sampling and testing, assesses any discrepancy, and is the only function that can move material out of quarantine."
      }
    }
  ]
